eing dark or camouflaged and i had  never noticed it either  so this was a real eye-opener for us and  really what  spurred a lot of this work and i'll talk  a little bit more about cr1 here in a  minute i want to briefly focus on  smn1 and smn2 which are  genes that are known to cause spinal  muscular atrophy  and they've also been implicated in  amylotrophic lateral  sclerosis or als so  smn1 and smn2 are over 90  camouflaged by again by each other  and what i'm showing in this figure is  a histogram of coverage  from a large data set known as the from  the nomad consortium  so this this data set has uh  nearly 150 000 whole genomes  and whole egg zones primarily exomes  and this is showing a histogram from  their data of the coverage for this  gene smn1 and the reason i'm showing  this is i want to just emphasize this  isn't just a problem in some of  our data it wasn't a technical artifact  from  from our sequencing this is ubiquitous  across all short read data sets  so smn1 and smn2 are nearly completely  camouflaged  similarly cr1 again as i mentioned a  really important gene in alzheimer's  disease is the top five gene  uh over approximately i guess exactly 20  of it is is dark or in this case it's  actually  camouflaged and what's unique about cr1  is it's camouflaged by itself  uh because within the gene there's a  tandem repeat of the c3b c4b binding  domain  and according to hg38 it's repeated  approximately three times and uh  that creates the alignment issue that we  previously discussed  so this was you know again a real eye  opener for us  so okay hopefully uh you know to this  point i've i've  convinced you that there are there are  some you know  holes some dark and camouflaged regions  that need to be addressed so what can we  do about it  um well there are kind of two approaches  one  is is one that we came up with for  existing short read data sets and i'm  just going to briefly describe that so  that you're aware that it exists  but i want to e